Hash-based flow steering (RSS in NICs, ECMP in routers) distributes traffic by hashing packet 5-tuples to assign flows to CPU cores or network paths. Because senders pick ephemeral source ports randomly, the resulting flow-to-core mapping is unpredictable, and collisions concentrate work on a single core, breaking per-flow pacing in long fat networks. At 400 Gbps, where no single TCP stream can saturate the link and parallel paced flows are mandatory, this collision penalty dominates throughput variability. We present SRAP (Sender-side Receiver-Aware Port selection), a user-space mechanism that exploits hash determinism: a small daemon exports the receiver’s RSS state, the sender precomputes a port → queue → core map, and an LD_PRELOAD binder steers each new TCP connection to a port that lands on a free core. No kernel, driver, or NIC firmware change is required, and any unmodified dynamically-linked TCP application benefits transparently. On a 400 Gbps point-to-point testbed, SRAP delivers a tight per-flow throughput distribution (IQR ≤ 8.7 pp) centered at 96–100% of the pacing target across three (n, r) configurations, while random ports show comparable medians (94–100%) but per-flow IQR widens from 0.7 to 18.7 pp as per-flow rates rise—the dispersion that breaks per-flow pacing. irqbalance stays at 36–45%, confirming that interrupt-level rebalancing cannot separate flows already sharing an RX queue.